    "lawCode": "Amd §§9.29 & 9.39, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3650\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to involuntary\nadmittance\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to respect a patient's choice of facility\nwhen being admitted involuntarily for treatment.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 9.29 of the mental hygiene law to require\nthat when there is an involuntary admittance to an in-patient facility\nthe person's or the person's guardian's choice of facility shall be\nrespected. However, if the facility cannot provide an appropriate\nlevel of care or there is no bed available the patient's choice can be\nwaived or if the health and safety of the individual would be\njeopardized.\n\nSection 2 amend section 9.39 of the mental hygiene law to require that\nwhen there is an involuntary admittance to an in-patient facility the\nperson's or the person's guardian's choice of facility shall be\nrespected. However, if the facility cannot provide an appropriate\nlevel of care or there is no bed available the patient's choice can be\nwaived or if the health and safety of the individual would be\njeopardize.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAn individual with a mental illness should continue to have a choice\nin where they will receive services. An individual may have received\nservices in the past at a certain facility where they may have the\nmost success receiving services. Upon involuntary admittance the\nindividual's choice should be upheld. However, in certain\ncircumstances a person's choice may not be appropriate or may not meet\nthe level of care they may require. The bill provides for\ncircumstances in which a person's choice may be waived. These\ncircumstances include when the facility of choice for a patient is not\nappropriate to meet the level of care that is needed or there is\ncurrently not a bed available for the person. In these cases the\nperson's right of choice can be waived, in order to ensure the person\nreceives immediate and appropriate care.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill. S5453A. Referred to Mental health and Disabilities.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\n\nTakes effect on the 120th day after it becomes law.  Effective\nimmediately is the addition, amendment, or repeal of any rule or\nregulation necessary for the implementation of this act on its\neffective date are authorized and directed to be made and completed on\nor before such effective date.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNegligible.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\n120th day.\n\n",
